1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:02,830
Twenty-five billion dollars.

2
00:00:02,830 --> 00:00:04,341
That is how much was traded on

3
00:00:04,341 --> 00:00:06,797
event contracts in America in March twenty

4
00:00:06,797 --> 00:00:07,533
twenty-six,

5
00:00:07,533 --> 00:00:10,400
according to the federal regulator.

6
00:00:10,530 --> 00:00:12,059
Contracts on elections,

7
00:00:12,059 --> 00:00:13,588
on interest rates,

8
00:00:13,588 --> 00:00:14,674
and above all,

9
00:00:14,674 --> 00:00:16,160
on sports games.

10
00:00:16,417 --> 00:00:18,457
Here is the twist.

11
00:00:18,617 --> 00:00:20,834
The platforms that run these markets say

12
00:00:20,834 --> 00:00:23,051
they are not betting against you at

13
00:00:23,051 --> 00:00:23,897
all.

14
00:00:23,927 --> 00:00:25,927
They do not need you to lose.

15
00:00:25,927 --> 00:00:28,142
They just need you to trade.

16
00:00:28,383 --> 00:00:30,588
So how does a market for hunches

17
00:00:30,588 --> 00:00:31,923
make money?

18
00:00:31,933 --> 00:00:33,214
And is it finance,

19
00:00:33,214 --> 00:00:34,900
or is it gambling?

20
00:00:35,150 --> 00:00:37,022
By the end of this video,

21
00:00:37,022 --> 00:00:39,393
you will know how a yes-or-no contract

22
00:00:39,393 --> 00:00:39,843
works,

23
00:00:39,843 --> 00:00:41,241
where the fee hides,

24
00:00:41,241 --> 00:00:43,138
why most of the money is on

25
00:00:43,138 --> 00:00:43,588
sports,

26
00:00:43,588 --> 00:00:45,721
and why the courts cannot agree on

27
00:00:45,721 --> 00:00:47,506
what these markets are.

28
00:00:53,150 --> 00:00:55,750
Start with the product.

29
00:00:56,020 --> 00:00:58,947
An event contract is a simple question

30
00:00:58,947 --> 00:01:01,410
with a yes or no answer.

31
00:01:01,760 --> 00:01:03,990
Will this team win tonight?

32
00:01:04,070 --> 00:01:06,419
Will the central bank cut rates next

33
00:01:06,419 --> 00:01:07,280
month?

34
00:01:07,450 --> 00:01:09,418
If the answer is yes,

35
00:01:09,418 --> 00:01:12,300
a yes contract pays one dollar.

36
00:01:12,370 --> 00:01:14,205
If the answer is no,

37
00:01:14,205 --> 00:01:15,869
it pays nothing.

38
00:01:16,117 --> 00:01:17,802
Before the event,

39
00:01:17,802 --> 00:01:20,372
people trade these contracts at prices

40
00:01:20,372 --> 00:01:23,257
between zero and one dollar.

41
00:01:23,607 --> 00:01:26,176
If a yes contract trades at sixty-five

42
00:01:26,176 --> 00:01:26,619
cents,

43
00:01:26,619 --> 00:01:27,764
the crowd is saying,

44
00:01:27,764 --> 00:01:28,441
roughly,

45
00:01:28,441 --> 00:01:31,009
that the event has a sixty-five percent

46
00:01:31,009 --> 00:01:32,777
chance of happening.

47
00:01:32,797 --> 00:01:35,225
The price is the prediction.

48
00:01:35,483 --> 00:01:37,523
And here is the key difference from

49
00:01:37,523 --> 00:01:38,783
a sportsbook.

50
00:01:38,783 --> 00:01:39,448
Kalshi,

51
00:01:39,448 --> 00:01:41,261
the largest of these platforms,

52
00:01:41,261 --> 00:01:43,555
says that you are always trading against

53
00:01:43,555 --> 00:01:45,826
another member of the platform,

54
00:01:45,826 --> 00:01:47,803
not the exchange itself.

55
00:01:47,893 --> 00:01:49,463
Someone buys yes.

56
00:01:49,463 --> 00:01:51,363
Someone else buys no.

57
00:01:51,363 --> 00:01:53,933
The exchange matches them in the middle.

58
00:01:54,183 --> 00:01:56,675
That is why Kalshi calls itself an

59
00:01:56,675 --> 00:01:57,478
exchange,

60
00:01:57,478 --> 00:01:59,463
not a bookmaker.

61
00:01:59,633 --> 00:02:00,819
In twenty twenty,

62
00:02:00,819 --> 00:02:03,615
the Commodity Futures Trading Commission,

63
00:02:03,615 --> 00:02:06,028
the regulator that oversees futures

64
00:02:06,028 --> 00:02:06,611
markets,

65
00:02:06,611 --> 00:02:09,423
designated it as a contract market.

66
00:02:09,503 --> 00:02:11,256
The same kind of license that big

67
00:02:11,256 --> 00:02:13,389
futures exchanges hold.

68
00:02:13,650 --> 00:02:15,890
Let me draw one contract.

69
00:02:15,890 --> 00:02:17,410
Here is the question.

70
00:02:17,830 --> 00:02:18,873
On the left,

71
00:02:18,873 --> 00:02:20,183
a buyer of yes,

72
00:02:20,183 --> 00:02:22,120
paying sixty-five cents.

73
00:02:22,310 --> 00:02:23,327
On the right,

74
00:02:23,327 --> 00:02:24,606
a buyer of no,

75
00:02:24,606 --> 00:02:26,510
paying thirty-five cents.

76
00:02:26,700 --> 00:02:27,397
Together,

77
00:02:27,397 --> 00:02:29,070
they put in one dollar.

78
00:02:29,150 --> 00:02:30,500
When the answer comes,

79
00:02:30,500 --> 00:02:32,820
one side takes the whole dollar.

80
00:02:32,820 --> 00:02:35,156
The exchange simply holds the money in

81
00:02:35,156 --> 00:02:36,224
between.

82
00:02:36,483 --> 00:02:39,002
So if the exchange never takes a

83
00:02:39,002 --> 00:02:39,480
side,

84
00:02:39,480 --> 00:02:41,844
where does its money come from?

85
00:02:52,483 --> 00:02:54,863
The answer is a toll.

86
00:02:55,253 --> 00:02:56,439
In its own words,

87
00:02:56,439 --> 00:02:58,861
Kalshi makes money by charging a

88
00:02:58,861 --> 00:03:01,524
transaction fee on the expected earnings

89
00:03:01,524 --> 00:03:03,093
on the contract.

90
00:03:03,443 --> 00:03:05,273
Not on who wins.

91
00:03:05,303 --> 00:03:07,209
On every trade,

92
00:03:07,209 --> 00:03:08,778
win or lose.

93
00:03:09,017 --> 00:03:12,007
The formula it filed with the regulator

94
00:03:12,007 --> 00:03:14,577
in twenty twenty-two looks like this.

95
00:03:14,577 --> 00:03:15,793
Seven percent,

96
00:03:15,793 --> 00:03:18,003
times the number of contracts,

97
00:03:18,003 --> 00:03:19,219
times the price,

98
00:03:19,219 --> 00:03:21,557
times one minus the price.

99
00:03:21,617 --> 00:03:23,566
At a price of fifty cents,

100
00:03:23,566 --> 00:03:25,540
that works out to about one point

101
00:03:25,540 --> 00:03:27,490
seven five cents per contract,

102
00:03:27,490 --> 00:03:29,817
rounded up to the next cent.

103
00:03:29,907 --> 00:03:32,192
There is no fee when the contract

104
00:03:32,192 --> 00:03:33,364
settles.

105
00:03:33,617 --> 00:03:36,337
Notice the shape of that formula.

106
00:03:36,367 --> 00:03:38,453
The fee is biggest when the price

107
00:03:38,453 --> 00:03:39,730
is fifty cents,

108
00:03:39,730 --> 00:03:41,816
when the outcome looks like a coin

109
00:03:41,816 --> 00:03:42,677
flip.

110
00:03:42,837 --> 00:03:44,847
It shrinks near zero and near one

111
00:03:44,847 --> 00:03:45,576
dollar,

112
00:03:45,576 --> 00:03:48,437
when the result is almost certain.

113
00:03:48,627 --> 00:03:50,398
The platform earns the most on the

114
00:03:50,398 --> 00:03:52,990
questions people are least sure about.

115
00:03:53,250 --> 00:03:54,299
Polymarket,

116
00:03:54,299 --> 00:03:55,562
its biggest rival,

117
00:03:55,562 --> 00:03:57,702
uses the same kind of formula for

118
00:03:57,702 --> 00:03:59,800
its American exchange.

119
00:04:00,180 --> 00:04:02,564
In a filing with the regulator,

120
00:04:02,564 --> 00:04:04,314
its rate for traders who take an

121
00:04:04,314 --> 00:04:06,939
existing offer rises to almost seven

122
00:04:06,939 --> 00:04:09,070
percent of the same price term.

123
00:04:09,450 --> 00:04:11,771
And traders who post offers and wait

124
00:04:11,771 --> 00:04:13,660
get a small rebate.

125
00:04:14,070 --> 00:04:16,486
Both platforms pay people to keep the

126
00:04:16,486 --> 00:04:17,891
market full.

127
00:04:18,150 --> 00:04:21,010
Those people are called market makers.

128
00:04:21,010 --> 00:04:23,461
They post prices on both sides and,

129
00:04:23,461 --> 00:04:24,922
as Kalshi puts it,

130
00:04:24,922 --> 00:04:27,750
make money by collecting the spread.

131
00:04:28,050 --> 00:04:29,755
And Kalshi has said that one of

132
00:04:29,755 --> 00:04:32,313
its own affiliates trades on the exchange

133
00:04:32,313 --> 00:04:32,718
too,

134
00:04:32,718 --> 00:04:34,810
mainly as a spread trader.

135
00:04:35,150 --> 00:04:36,962
So the exchange itself is not your

136
00:04:36,962 --> 00:04:37,747
opponent,

137
00:04:37,747 --> 00:04:39,358
but the other side of your trade

138
00:04:39,358 --> 00:04:41,368
may be a professional.

139
00:04:41,617 --> 00:04:44,127
Let me draw the toll curve.

140
00:04:44,297 --> 00:04:45,611
Along the bottom,

141
00:04:45,611 --> 00:04:46,258
the price,

142
00:04:46,258 --> 00:04:48,417
from zero to one dollar.

143
00:04:48,617 --> 00:04:49,642
Up the side,

144
00:04:49,642 --> 00:04:50,767
the fee.

145
00:04:50,947 --> 00:04:52,482
It starts near zero,

146
00:04:52,482 --> 00:04:54,797
climbs to its peak at fifty cents,

147
00:04:54,797 --> 00:04:56,437
and falls again.

148
00:04:56,517 --> 00:04:57,226
A hill,

149
00:04:57,226 --> 00:04:59,158
with the toll booth at the top,

150
00:04:59,158 --> 00:05:00,871
right where the hunches are most

151
00:05:00,871 --> 00:05:02,204
uncertain.

152
00:05:02,450 --> 00:05:04,622
A small toll on a huge number

153
00:05:04,622 --> 00:05:06,580
of trades adds up.

154
00:05:06,700 --> 00:05:09,034
And the biggest source of those trades

155
00:05:09,034 --> 00:05:10,931
is not politics.

156
00:05:16,583 --> 00:05:19,526
The platforms do not publish an official

157
00:05:19,526 --> 00:05:20,393
split.

158
00:05:20,633 --> 00:05:23,557
But the Pew Research Center analyzed data

159
00:05:23,557 --> 00:05:25,783
compiled by a crypto news site,

160
00:05:25,783 --> 00:05:26,833
The Block.

161
00:05:27,073 --> 00:05:29,444
In July twenty twenty-six,

162
00:05:29,444 --> 00:05:31,842
sports were the most traded topic on

163
00:05:31,842 --> 00:05:32,881
both platforms,

164
00:05:32,881 --> 00:05:34,813
by a large margin.

165
00:05:34,933 --> 00:05:37,734
By our calculation from that table,

166
00:05:37,734 --> 00:05:40,304
about four out of five dollars traded

167
00:05:40,304 --> 00:05:41,675
were on sports.

168
00:05:41,917 --> 00:05:44,194
The growth claims are enormous,

169
00:05:44,194 --> 00:05:45,947
but they are claims.

170
00:05:46,217 --> 00:05:47,295
In October,

171
00:05:47,295 --> 00:05:49,936
Kalshi said its annualized trading volume

172
00:05:49,936 --> 00:05:51,895
now exceeds one trillion dollars,

173
00:05:51,895 --> 00:05:54,316
and that it handles over seventy-five

174
00:05:54,316 --> 00:05:57,117
percent of prediction market trading.

175
00:05:57,377 --> 00:05:59,398
It raised one billion dollars at a

176
00:05:59,398 --> 00:06:01,817
forty billion dollar valuation.

177
00:06:01,967 --> 00:06:04,136
None of those numbers is an audited

178
00:06:04,136 --> 00:06:05,170
figure.

179
00:06:05,417 --> 00:06:07,257
Wall Street noticed.

180
00:06:07,257 --> 00:06:09,296
The company that owns the New York

181
00:06:09,296 --> 00:06:10,179
Stock Exchange,

182
00:06:10,179 --> 00:06:12,195
Intercontinental Exchange,

183
00:06:12,195 --> 00:06:14,007
said it would invest up to two

184
00:06:14,007 --> 00:06:16,250
billion dollars in Polymarket,

185
00:06:16,250 --> 00:06:18,742
at a valuation of about eight billion

186
00:06:18,742 --> 00:06:21,154
dollars before the investment.

187
00:06:21,417 --> 00:06:24,397
And the sportsbooks joined in.

188
00:06:24,557 --> 00:06:26,639
DraftKings launched its own prediction

189
00:06:26,639 --> 00:06:29,391
product in December twenty twenty-five,

190
00:06:29,391 --> 00:06:31,242
with its own exchange and its own

191
00:06:31,242 --> 00:06:32,767
market maker.

192
00:06:33,037 --> 00:06:35,219
FanDuel launched one with CME Group,

193
00:06:35,219 --> 00:06:37,155
the giant futures exchange,

194
00:06:37,155 --> 00:06:39,852
in states where regular sports betting is

195
00:06:39,852 --> 00:06:41,187
not legal.

196
00:06:41,617 --> 00:06:44,629
The NBA named Kalshi and Polymarket its

197
00:06:44,629 --> 00:06:47,989
preferred prediction market exchanges.

198
00:06:48,250 --> 00:06:51,050
Let me draw where the money goes.

199
00:06:51,160 --> 00:06:52,892
Here is one big circle,

200
00:06:52,892 --> 00:06:55,170
all the trading in July.

201
00:06:55,170 --> 00:06:56,520
Now cut it.

202
00:06:56,520 --> 00:06:58,325
A thin slice for politics,

203
00:06:58,325 --> 00:07:01,210
economics and everything else.

204
00:07:01,280 --> 00:07:02,490
And the rest,

205
00:07:02,490 --> 00:07:04,010
about four fifths,

206
00:07:04,010 --> 00:07:05,230
is sports.

207
00:07:05,250 --> 00:07:06,951
A market for hunches,

208
00:07:06,951 --> 00:07:09,524
mostly about who wins the game.

209
00:07:09,783 --> 00:07:12,183
Which raises the obvious question.

210
00:07:12,223 --> 00:07:14,063
If it looks like sports betting,

211
00:07:14,063 --> 00:07:16,393
why is it not regulated like sports

212
00:07:16,393 --> 00:07:17,458
betting?

213
00:07:23,117 --> 00:07:25,957
The rules have not always been friendly.

214
00:07:25,957 --> 00:07:27,310
In twenty twenty-two,

215
00:07:27,310 --> 00:07:30,253
the regulator ordered Polymarket to pay a

216
00:07:30,253 --> 00:07:32,410
one point four million dollars penalty for

217
00:07:32,410 --> 00:07:34,372
running what it called an illegal

218
00:07:34,372 --> 00:07:36,737
unregistered facility.

219
00:07:36,847 --> 00:07:38,857
Then the climate changed.

220
00:07:38,857 --> 00:07:40,727
In July twenty twenty-five,

221
00:07:40,727 --> 00:07:43,039
the company that now operates as

222
00:07:43,039 --> 00:07:45,560
Polymarket US was designated as a

223
00:07:45,560 --> 00:07:47,421
regulated exchange.

224
00:07:47,683 --> 00:07:50,753
Then the states pushed back.

225
00:07:50,993 --> 00:07:54,285
Sports betting in America is regulated by

226
00:07:54,285 --> 00:07:54,967
each state,

227
00:07:54,967 --> 00:07:57,683
with its own licenses and taxes.

228
00:07:57,883 --> 00:08:00,545
Several states told the platforms that

229
00:08:00,545 --> 00:08:03,207
their sports contracts were simply illegal

230
00:08:03,207 --> 00:08:04,073
bets.

231
00:08:04,353 --> 00:08:06,370
The platforms answered that they are

232
00:08:06,370 --> 00:08:09,037
federally regulated exchanges,

233
00:08:09,037 --> 00:08:11,205
and that states have no say.

234
00:08:11,450 --> 00:08:13,210
The courts split.

235
00:08:13,370 --> 00:08:15,391
In April twenty twenty-six,

236
00:08:15,391 --> 00:08:17,663
a federal appeals court in the Third

237
00:08:17,663 --> 00:08:19,457
Circuit sided with Kalshi,

238
00:08:19,457 --> 00:08:20,343
two to one,

239
00:08:20,343 --> 00:08:22,760
at an early stage of the case.

240
00:08:22,920 --> 00:08:24,739
It said the sports contracts are swaps

241
00:08:24,739 --> 00:08:26,990
traded on a licensed exchange,

242
00:08:26,990 --> 00:08:29,945
under the federal regulator's exclusive

243
00:08:29,945 --> 00:08:31,000
control.

244
00:08:31,270 --> 00:08:32,737
One judge disagreed,

245
00:08:32,737 --> 00:08:35,719
and called that position a performative

246
00:08:35,719 --> 00:08:36,568
sleight.

247
00:08:36,817 --> 00:08:38,026
Then in August,

248
00:08:38,026 --> 00:08:39,235
the Ninth Circuit,

249
00:08:39,235 --> 00:08:41,184
in a case from Nevada,

250
00:08:41,184 --> 00:08:43,017
went the other way.

251
00:08:43,087 --> 00:08:45,500
It said the contracts were not swaps,

252
00:08:45,500 --> 00:08:47,727
because they were sports bets.

253
00:08:48,017 --> 00:08:49,303
Two appeals courts,

254
00:08:49,303 --> 00:08:51,477
two opposite answers.

255
00:08:51,717 --> 00:08:54,125
Now the question is knocking at the

256
00:08:54,125 --> 00:08:55,527
Supreme Court.

257
00:08:55,627 --> 00:08:57,961
New Jersey has asked the justices to

258
00:08:57,961 --> 00:08:59,197
take the case.

259
00:08:59,287 --> 00:09:00,276
As of today,

260
00:09:00,276 --> 00:09:02,295
they have not decided whether to hear

261
00:09:02,295 --> 00:09:03,097
it.

262
00:09:03,507 --> 00:09:04,287
Meanwhile,

263
00:09:04,287 --> 00:09:06,687
the federal regulator has taken the

264
00:09:06,687 --> 00:09:07,887
platforms' side.

265
00:09:08,057 --> 00:09:10,116
It dropped a plan to ban sports

266
00:09:10,116 --> 00:09:12,149
and political contracts,

267
00:09:12,149 --> 00:09:15,065
and it has sued several states.

268
00:09:15,317 --> 00:09:18,017
Let me draw the tug of war.

269
00:09:18,017 --> 00:09:18,859
On one side,

270
00:09:18,859 --> 00:09:21,642
the platforms and the federal regulator,

271
00:09:21,642 --> 00:09:23,517
pulling toward finance.

272
00:09:23,517 --> 00:09:24,620
On the other side,

273
00:09:24,620 --> 00:09:26,845
the states and their gaming regulators,

274
00:09:26,845 --> 00:09:28,567
pulling toward betting.

275
00:09:28,637 --> 00:09:29,843
In the middle,

276
00:09:29,843 --> 00:09:30,556
the rope,

277
00:09:30,556 --> 00:09:32,771
a yes or no contract on tonight's

278
00:09:32,771 --> 00:09:33,617
game.

279
00:09:33,647 --> 00:09:35,324
And the referee has not blown the

280
00:09:35,324 --> 00:09:36,483
whistle yet.

281
00:09:36,717 --> 00:09:39,161
So whatever the courts decide,

282
00:09:39,161 --> 00:09:42,181
one part of this business is already

283
00:09:42,181 --> 00:09:43,056
clear.

284
00:09:48,717 --> 00:09:51,547
Do the math on a single trade.

285
00:09:51,597 --> 00:09:53,904
You buy one hundred contracts at fifty

286
00:09:53,904 --> 00:09:54,342
cents,

287
00:09:54,342 --> 00:09:56,557
fifty dollars in total.

288
00:09:56,697 --> 00:09:59,004
Under the formula Kalshi filed,

289
00:09:59,004 --> 00:10:00,909
the fee is about one dollar and

290
00:10:00,909 --> 00:10:02,567
seventy-five cents.

291
00:10:02,607 --> 00:10:03,479
If you win,

292
00:10:03,479 --> 00:10:05,857
you collect one hundred dollars.

293
00:10:05,857 --> 00:10:06,716
If you lose,

294
00:10:06,716 --> 00:10:08,197
you get nothing.

295
00:10:08,227 --> 00:10:09,181
Either way,

296
00:10:09,181 --> 00:10:11,494
the toll was paid at the start.

297
00:10:11,750 --> 00:10:13,224
For the whole market,

298
00:10:13,224 --> 00:10:15,359
the regulator put the numbers in

299
00:10:15,359 --> 00:10:16,600
perspective.

300
00:10:16,940 --> 00:10:19,805
Twenty-five billion dollars of event

301
00:10:19,805 --> 00:10:22,410
contracts in a month sounds huge.

302
00:10:22,690 --> 00:10:24,883
But it is about zero point zero

303
00:10:24,883 --> 00:10:27,295
eight percent of all the futures trading

304
00:10:27,295 --> 00:10:29,430
the commission oversees.

305
00:10:29,650 --> 00:10:30,765
Small for Wall Street,

306
00:10:30,765 --> 00:10:33,951
enormous for a product that barely existed

307
00:10:33,951 --> 00:10:35,688
a few years ago.

308
00:10:35,950 --> 00:10:38,470
Let me draw the business model.

309
00:10:38,590 --> 00:10:39,583
On one side,

310
00:10:39,583 --> 00:10:42,210
thousands of traders with hunches.

311
00:10:42,210 --> 00:10:43,328
In the middle,

312
00:10:43,328 --> 00:10:44,218
the exchange,

313
00:10:44,218 --> 00:10:47,100
taking a small toll on every match.

314
00:10:47,300 --> 00:10:48,607
On the other side,

315
00:10:48,607 --> 00:10:49,914
the market makers,

316
00:10:49,914 --> 00:10:51,400
earning the spread.

317
00:10:51,610 --> 00:10:53,361
The traders win and lose against each

318
00:10:53,361 --> 00:10:54,350
other.

319
00:10:54,380 --> 00:10:56,176
The toll booth is the only one

320
00:10:56,176 --> 00:10:59,020
that collects on every single trade.

321
00:10:59,283 --> 00:11:01,828
So how do prediction markets make money

322
00:11:01,828 --> 00:11:03,353
off your hunches?

323
00:11:03,383 --> 00:11:05,673
Not by betting against you.

324
00:11:05,893 --> 00:11:08,117
By charging a small fee every time

325
00:11:08,117 --> 00:11:08,762
you trade,

326
00:11:08,762 --> 00:11:10,763
most of all when nobody is sure

327
00:11:10,763 --> 00:11:12,253
what will happen.

328
00:11:12,353 --> 00:11:14,927
Whether that makes them a financial market

329
00:11:14,927 --> 00:11:17,072
or a casino is a question the

330
00:11:17,072 --> 00:11:19,173
courts are still fighting over.

331
00:11:19,223 --> 00:11:21,556
But the toll booth gets paid either

332
00:11:21,556 --> 00:11:22,448
way.

333
00:11:22,683 --> 00:11:24,685
Next time on The Hidden Economics,

334
00:11:24,685 --> 00:11:27,073
we follow the money behind another hidden

335
00:11:27,073 --> 00:11:29,013
link of the world economy.

336
00:11:29,013 --> 00:11:31,186
This is The Hidden Economics.
